const edges = `
<dialog class="sk-vaul" data-edge="inline-start">…</dialog>
<dialog class="sk-vaul" data-edge="inline-end">…</dialog>
<dialog class="sk-vaul" data-edge="block-end">…</dialog>`;

const vanilla = `import { connectVaul } from "@skryensya/vanilla/vaul";

// Just the drag. Open is showModal() and close is close():
// The modality is from the platform.
const cleanup = connectVaul(document.querySelector("#demo-vaul"), {
  // fraction of the panel to drag
  dismissThreshold: 0.4,
  // px/ms: a flick closes without crossing the distance
  dismissVelocity: 0.5,
  onOpenChange(details) {
    console.log(details.open);
  },
});`;

const noDrag = `<dialog
  class="sk-vaul"
  data-sk-vaul
  data-draggable="false"
  data-edge="block-end"
>
  <!-- without handle and no drag: Vaul is still complete -->
</dialog>`;
---

<Base title="Vaul" description="A pattern: modal panel anchored to a viewport edge, with optional drag-to-dismiss.">
  <h1>Vaul</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A modal panel anchored to a <strong>edge</strong> of the viewport: arrives from that edge, leaves the
    inert page behind a backdrop, and goes where it came from. The edge is the whole idea, a Vaul
    It is named by where it comes from, never by the shape it makes when it arrives.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={html} label="HTML" viewport="overlay" />

  <h2>Why is it a pattern and not a component?</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a single question:
    <em>could a second component need this exact structure?</em> Here the answer is not a
    prediction, it happens, today, in this repo: <a href="/en/components/drawer">Drawer</a> <strong>es</strong>
    a Vaul on the inline edge. The <a href="/en/components/dialog">Dialog Vaul</a> option reuses the same
    Block-end interaction on mobile without converting Dialog into a Vaul component.
  </p>
  <p>
    So Vaul sends <strong>hooks and structure</strong>. A drawer that reimplemented the panel would be
    a second Vaul with another name.
  </p>

  <h2>The edge is a data, not a component</h2>
  <p>
    Three edges, one pattern. <code>data-edge</code> choose; nothing else changes. The inline edges are
    logical, so they turn around on their own in RTL instead of sticking to the wrong side of the screen.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={edges} label="HTML" />

  <h2>Requires native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>
    The focus trap, ESC, the inert background, the focus restoration, the top layer and a
    Real <code>::backdrop</code> are behaviors that the platform already has. A Vaul over a div
    it reimplements them in JavaScript that would have to be sent, and the top layer and <code>:modal</code> are not
    available for a div at no price.
  </p>

  <h2>Zag is not used here, and that is the finding</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is that Zag enters only when the state coordination is worth its cost. The record was reviewed before
    write a line: <strong>no Vaul machine</strong> exists. And what Zag could cover is
    exactly what the platform already does best, <code>@zag-js/dialog</code> would reimplement the
    modality that <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> provides, and <code>@zag-js/presence</code> is redundant
    against <code>@starting-style</code>. That the answer is "none" is the working rule, not a
    exception.
  </p>

  <h2>Drag is the only thing that costs JS</h2>
  <p>
    There remains <strong>un</strong> behavior without an equivalent on the platform and without a machine in Zag:
    Drag the panel toward its edge to close it. That, and only that, is the enhancer.
    <strong>Vaul is complete without it</strong>: without JS there is panel, slide, backdrop, ESC and click outside.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={vanilla} label="JavaScript" />
  <p>
    It closes by distance <strong>or</strong> by speed: a flick is an intention, and waiting for
    crossing a distance threshold is exactly what makes a blade feel stuck. The speed
    is measured on the <strong>last millimeters</strong> of the gesture, not on the average: if you drag
    Slowly, you hesitate, and only then do you pull, the pull is real, averaged from when you put your finger down.
    dilute until it seems like you did not move. And a flick <em>back</em> beats him from distance: the
    last word of the hand is your word.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={noDrag} label="no drag" />

  <h2>For the other side, resist</h2>
  <p>
    Pulling the panel inwards does not detach it from its edge, but it does not leave it stuck either: it gives way
    starting 1∶1 under the bud and curves to a stop of about 12px. Let the finger move and the thing
    below is not the only moment in which a surface of direct manipulation reveals itself as a drawing
    of a surface. The asymmetry <em>is</em> the message: there is no path to that side.
  </p>
  <p>
    And what appears in that gap is <strong>plus panel</strong>: the material continues past the
    edge, so lifting a leaf reveals a leaf and not a strip of backdrop. They are two numbers
    different on purpose, how much it gives (<code>--sk-vaul-overpull</code>) and how far the
    material (<code>--sk-vaul-material</code>), and the second is declared as a floor above the first
    so that giving in can never win. Material is painted <em>above</em> elevation: at
    On the other hand, the shadow of the panel dyed just the strip that had just been revealed, and a raised leaf
    showed a dark band where its own surface should be, which is exactly the hole
    that this exists to not have.
  </p>
  <p>
    The backdrop goes with that: its opacity follows the drag. A full backdrop with the panel half out
    I would be lying about what modal is.
  </p>

  <h2>The handle is still on the edge</h2>
  <p>
    It is not cosmetic: a sash pulls down from a bar that crosses its roof, and a side panel
    It is pulled sideways from a bar along its inside edge. A horizontal pill in a drawer advertises
    the wrong gesture, and an affordance that lies is worse than having none.
  </p>

  <h2>On desktop it does not drag</h2>
  <p>
    The handle is a <strong>tactile</strong> affordance: a bar that is pulled with the thumb. in a pointer
    There is nothing to pull that a click away or an ESC will not do better, so above the breakpoint of the
    system (<code>52rem</code>, the same width where the sidebar becomes a drawer and the dialog becomes
    sheet) the handle is hidden and the enhancer does not wire the drag. CSS and JS make the same cut, with the
    same <code>matchMedia</code>, so there is never a handle dangling at a width where it does not drag.
  </p>

  <h2>Motion: arriving, leaving and releasing are three intentions</h2>
  <p>
    Each one with its tokens, never an average duration: entering is announced, leaving is already decided. with
    <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> the panel <strong>continues to arrive</strong>, the trip is dropped, not the
    Vaul, and the fade of the backdrop is what says that the page was inert. Drag is not touched: it is
    direct manipulation, no motion that the system reproduces for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/en/transparency"><code>prefers-reduced-transparency</code></a> does not remove the backdrop:
    makes it opaque. The modality and direct response to drag remain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Drop</strong> is the third, and it is its own: a dropped panel is not exiting, it is ending
    the impulse that the hand already gave him. That is why it does not consume <code>enter</code> or <code>exit</code>, both
    are short because no one expects them, but the <code>release</code> intent, with a long duration
    purpose: cutting it is what causes the panel to stop dead under your finger instead of continuing
    long. The same attempt for both destinations: returning home and leaving completely are the same shot with
    different ending. It is the only intent that under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> cannot release its
    trip, the panel is where your finger left it and it has to get somewhere, so let go of the luxury and
    lands short.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "patterns/vaul.css", probe: "sk-vaul" }]} />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* La piel del demo es del consumidor, como siempre: el pattern trae hooks y estructura, no pintura.
     El ancho lo decide el clamp del pattern; el alto lo da el contenido hasta el techo del sheet.
     `is:global`: el HTML autorado vive dentro del srcdoc y no lleva el atributo de scope de Astro;
     el adapter copia esta regla global al frame, una regla scopeada no podría alcanzarlo. */
  .demo-vaul {
    --sk-vaul-radius: var(--radius-surface) var(--radius-surface) 0 0;
    /* claim a real slice of the screen rather than hugging three rows at the bottom edge */
    min-block-size: min(64dvh, 32rem);

    /* El inset lateral de la hoja, en UN solo lugar: la cabecera, la fila del enlace, las filas de la
       lista y las acciones tienen que caer sobre la misma vertical, y son cuatro reglas distintas.
       Un pelo por encima de `md` y no `lg`: en una hoja angosta el salto a `lg` se nota como ancho
       perdido, mientras que estos 4px sólo despegan el texto del borde. */
    --demo-vaul-inset: calc(var(--space-inset-md) + var(--space-inset-xs));
  }

  /* El sheet ES la columna: header, fila de enlace, lista y acciones son sus hijos directos. Un
     wrapper interno sólo existiría para volver a declarar el mismo flex y para poner un padding que
     las filas ya traen. */
  .demo-vaul[open] {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* Un sheet se sostiene con una mano: cada fila de padding es una fila de contenido que no entró.
     Por eso el inset es corto y no `xl`, y por eso las bandas se separan con una línea en vez de con
     un `gap` grande, una línea cuesta 1px y dice lo mismo.
     Arriba sí se paga: el handle es una barra de 12px sin aire propio, y con la cabecera pegada a
     ella el título parecía colgar del borde en vez de empezar la hoja. */
  .demo-vaul__header {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-inline-md);
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: var(--space-inset-sm) var(--demo-vaul-inset);
  }

  .demo-vaul__heading {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .demo-vaul__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-heading-sm);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-heading);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-heading);
  }

  .demo-vaul__meta {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
  }

  .demo-vaul__link {
    flex: none;
    padding: var(--space-inset-sm) var(--demo-vaul-inset);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
  }

  /* La lista se lleva el alto sobrante y scrollea sola: header, enlace y acciones se quedan quietos
     mientras el dedo recorre las personas.
     El inset lateral NO se pone acá: las filas ya traen el suyo, así que se les pasa el de la hoja
     por su hook y siguen corriendo de borde a borde. Un padding en la lista las metería hacia adentro
     y dejaría el divisor corto. */
  .demo-vaul__people {
    --sk-list-row-padding-x: var(--demo-vaul-inset);

    flex: 1;
    min-block-size: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    overscroll-behavior: contain;
  }

  .demo-vaul__actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-inline-sm);
    justify-content: flex-end;
    padding: var(--space-inset-sm) var(--demo-vaul-inset);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
  }
</style>
